{"id": "1c037e8c-6c52-4975-9fd8-a70e4b51031f", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"id": "tooluse_AcMamu7JETm7DajpGc8a8L", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-cost-change-analysis", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Explain why the Amazon EBS bill jumped this month. Identify which cost component(s) (volume storage, provisioned IOPS, provisioned throughput, snapshots) drove the increase, and which resources/changes caused it, with dates.",
  "context": "User says 'My EBS bill jumped this month and I don't know why. What changed?' Today is 2026-11-11 (the current month, November 2026, is only ~10 days in, so compare DAILY run-rate / daily averages, not month totals, against prior full months). Agent space account is 012345678901_1, home region us-east-1. This is READ-ONLY work: make no mutating calls. Use the account's own Cost Explorer amounts; never type list prices.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 012345678901_1 (and any other associated accounts if list_associations shows them)", "Cost Explorer (us-east-1 endpoint)", "EC2 EBS volumes and snapshots in every region that shows EBS usage"],
  "timeframe": "2026-07-01 through 2026-11-11 (monthly trend for context, daily granularity for Oct-Nov 2026)",
  "criteria": "Procedure: (1) Cost Explorer GetCostAndUsage, monthly for the last ~4 months and daily for Oct-Nov, grouped by USAGE_TYPE (EBS charges appear under service 'EC2 - Other'; confirm via GetDimensionValues SERVICE). Also group by REGION if helpful. (2) GetDimensionValues for USAGE_TYPE and classify EBS usage types by name: VolumeUsage (storage), IOPS (provisioned IOPS), Throughput (provisioned throughput), Snapshot (snapshots; also note EBS fast snapshot restore / direct API usage types if present). (3) Report, per component, the account's own amounts for the two compared periods (normalize to daily average) and the delta; name the component that drove the jump and the date it started (find the day in the daily series where the step happened). (4) In the regions with EBS usage, find what changed: ec2 describe_volumes_modifications (original vs target size/type/IOPS/throughput with start times; gp2->gp3 or gp3->io1/io2 changes, IOPS/throughput increases), describe_volumes (CreateTime for volumes created since ~Oct 1; State=available for unattached/idle volumes; size, type, iops, throughput), describe_snapshots OwnerIds=self (count/size growth, snapshots created since Oct 1, very old ones), DLM get_lifecycle_policies and AWS Backup list_backup_plans for policies creating/retaining snapshots, Recycle Bin list_rules (resources in Recycle Bin are billed during retention). If DLM/Recycle Bin reads are blocked behind an approval gate, report as a Gap, do not bypass. CloudTrail is optional; do not rely on it (use lookup_cloudtrail_events only if available, otherwise note a Gap). (5) Brief right-sizing check for volumes with provisioned IOPS/throughput above gp3 baseline (3,000 IOPS / 125 MiB/s): compare to measured usage from CloudWatch AWS/EBS (IOPS = Sum(VolumeReadOps+VolumeWriteOps)/period seconds; MiB/s = Sum(VolumeReadBytes+VolumeWriteBytes)/period/1048576) over the last 7 days. Report units in MiB/s. Note: gp3 includes 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s at any size; only amounts above are billed as provisioned performance. Snapshot savings are an upper bound because snapshots are incremental. Also note (as 'Separate findings', briefly) idle/unattached volumes. Return a concise report: Answer first (component + resource + date that drove the jump), Evidence table (component, prior daily avg, current daily avg, delta, in the account's currency), change records explaining each, optional proposals (resource, exact parameters, cost direction, rollback), and Gaps (any API that failed/was denied/not checked)."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Analyze EBS cost change across components and change records"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "19c52dff-ca7f-4223-a6c2-e2cef65c0c8c", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm noticing the io2 IOPS costs in us-east-1 climbed steadily from July through October before November's partial data shows a slight dip in daily rate, and I'm also seeing Canada and EU regions appear in July/August data before disappearing later.

The November Cost Explorer data appears to only run through roughly day 10, so I need to pull daily usage broken out by usage type across all regions for October 1 through November 12 to compute real daily averages and find where the actual spike occurred.

"}], "parent_id": "77b8a331-733a-4db6-9dc1-b52369bda4bc"}

{"id": "e79d9454-e9a3-4de7-9c43-1891329b4a28", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "DLM and recycle bin look gated, so I'll flag those as gaps. Other regions show no volumes now since the Jul 29–Aug 19 resources are gone, and the August spike was CAN1/EU/EUC1 io2 volumes that got deleted around Aug 20, plus the USW2 Nov 5 volume is also gone — I should check modifications and snapshots in us-west-2, and also look into whether the "Trial" backup plan might be creating EBS snapshots.

Now I'm pulling CloudWatch metrics for right-sizing: vol-0917 (io2, 20000 IOPS, long-lived, the main cost driver) needs 7-day IOPS analysis, while vol-00612 and the newer volumes only exist for a short window so I'll query what data is available. I'm setting up a get_metric_data call across these volumes for the past 7 days with hourly periods, computing total IOPS via read+write sums divided by time, then using jmespath to pull max and average values per volume.

"}], "parent_id": "77b8a331-733a-4db6-9dc1-b52369bda4bc"}

{"id": "cc997dc9-6c06-4d62-b586-d0783a673eb3", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "vol-0917 stands out: it's provisioned as io2 at 20,000 IOPS but the measured max hourly average is only 0.029 IOPS, meaning it's essentially idle and the real cost driver here. It's attached alongside an st1 125GiB and gp3 10GiB volume on the same instance, all created together on 2026-06-26, so I want to check its modification history and whether the IOPS-to-GiB ratio for io2 (max 500 IOPS per GiB) is actually justified.

The io2 IOPS ratio checks out at 1000/GiB for Block Express, so that's not misconfigured relative to size—just massively overprovisioned for actual usage. Given the IOPS alone likely cost around $1300/mo, I'm thinking the fix is converting io2 to gp3, which includes 3000 IOPS and 125MiB/s baseline for free and would eliminate that IOPS charge entirely; Elastic Volumes supports this modification, and gp3's 16,000 IOPS ceiling isn't a constraint here. I should also quickly check whether the attached instance is even running, since a stopped instance would mean this volume is doubly idle, and I want to see if CloudTrail shows any recent ModifyVolume events to understand who last touched this configuration.

"}], "parent_id": "77b8a331-733a-4db6-9dc1-b52369bda4bc"}

{"id": "b4ff721b-741e-424b-8894-681f5faee4c7", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I couldn't locate the ModifyVolume event for Sep 18, possibly due to naming differences or lookup delay, so I'll flag that as a gap and skip checking eu-central-1 for now. I'm computing daily EBS cost averages across October and early November to establish a baseline for comparison.

Breaking this down by component, the October-to-November daily deltas are mostly small (io2 IOPS, gp3, storage, snapshots all shift modestly), with the overall increase of about 3.3% explained mostly by the 31-to-30 day proration rather than real usage change. The more significant shifts are the step changes: io2 IOPS jumped ~33% around Sep 18/19, and there was a large spike (+171/day) between Jul 29–Aug 19, visible in the monthly totals (Jul 1367, Aug 4326, Sep 1129, Oct 1323, Nov MTD ~434 with a run-rate suggesting ~1300+ for the full month). I'm noting to double check for any new Nov 11 costs and confirming the Cost Explorer figures are in UnblendedCost currency terms before writing this up concisely.

"}], "parent_id": "77b8a331-733a-4db6-9dc1-b52369bda4bc"}

